    kg:textSpan ", A i , Y i ) , where X i is a vector of confounding variables, that is factors that inuence simultaneously the potential outcomes and treatment. We assume that the data are an independent identically distributed sample of size N . Individual level causal eects are dened as the dierence between these potential outcomes. 3 Researchers are often interested in the average of these individual causal eects over some population. A widely considered casual estimand is the ATE, dened as = E [ Y 1 i Y 0 " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1903_00402_rag__chunk_004> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1903.00402_rag::chunk_004" ;
    kg:docId "1903.00402_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1903_00402_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "a subset of the observation from the model tting process, using the so-called V-fold cross-validation procedure (see e.g. Zhang (1993)). When performing V -fold cross-validation, the researcher randomly divides the set of observations into V groups (folds). The 6 rst group is withheld from the tting process, and thus referred to as the test data . The algorithm is then tted using the data in the remaining V 1 folds, called the training data . Finally, the MSE is calculated using the test data, t" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1903_00402_rag__chunk_010> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1903.00402_rag::chunk_010" ;
    kg:docId "1903.00402_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1903_00402_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "minimises a chosen loss function, based on covariate balance achieved in the IPTW 15 weighted data, by iteratively forming a collection of simple regression tree models and adding them together to estimate the propensity score. It models directly the log-odds of treatment rather than the propensity scores, to simplify computations. The algorithm can be specied to stop when the best balance is achieved. A recommended stopping rule is the average standardised absolute mean dierence (ASAM) in the c" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1903_00402_rag__chunk_019> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1903.00402_rag::chunk_019" ;
    kg:docId "1903.00402_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1903_00402_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "a result, mistakenly excluding variables with non-zero coecients. To address these challenges, Belloni et al. (2014b) proposed a solution that oers principled 29 variable selection, taking into account both the covariate-outcome and the covariate-treatment assignment association, resulting in valid inferences after variable selection. Their framework, referred to as post double selection, or double-lasso, also allows to extend to space of possible confounding variables to include higher order te" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1903_00402_rag__chunk_020> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1903.00402_rag::chunk_020" ;
    kg:docId "1903.00402_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1903_00402_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "outcomes, and obtain the ATE. We also calculate IPTW and AIPTW estimates using the weights from a parametric logistic model for the PS. For all estimates we use bootstrap to obtain SEs. We then increase the covariate space to include all the two-way interactions between the covariates, excluding the exposure and the outcome, resulting in a total of 595 covariates. Using double-lasso on this extended covariate space, we select 156 covariates for the outcome and 89 for the treatment model, leaving" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1903_00402_rag__chunk_022> a kg:Chunk ;
