    kg:textSpan "1996 ]) and other supple- mentary analyses ( Athey and Imbens [ 2017 ], Ding et al. [ 2017 ]). For example, in the seminal restriction (the absence of a direct eect of the instrument, the draft lottery number, on the outcome, earnings) for never-takers and always-takers, because it may be much more plausible for some groups than for others. Such discussions underline how rare it is in practice to have settings where the absence of arrows is immediately credible. A closely related setting where D" .

