    kg:textSpan "also discuss identication strategies for cases when confounding bias cannot be eliminated in purely observational data, but in which a surrogate experiment (akin to an instrumental variable that creates exogenous variation in a treatment) is available. 3.1. Covariate selection and the backdoor criterion Consider the well-known example from labour economics of estimating the college wage premium (Angrist and Pischke, 2009, ch. 3.2.3). Let the causal relationships in the problem be represented by " .

