    kg:textSpan "Thus, large-scale observational datasets hold open the promise of a much greater impact for causal inference methodology. However, conceptual problems arise in the observational data setting which can make causal eects unidentiable or hard to estimate. The problems include unmeasured confounding, noisy measurements, inconsistency, and selection bias [ Rosenbaum and Rubin , 1983 , Angrist et al. , 1996 , Nalatore et al. , 2007 , Hernan et al. , 2004 ]. These problems have generally been studied i" .

