    kg:textSpan "Estimating individual treatment effect: generalization bounds and algorithms Uri Shalit* SHALIT @ CS . NYU . EDU CIMS, New York University, New York, NY 10003 Fredrik D. Johansson* FREDRIKJ @ MIT . EDU IMES, MIT, Cambridge, MA 02142 arXiv:1606.03976v5 [stat.ML] 16 May 2017 David Sontag DSONTAG @ CSAIL . MIT . EDU CSAIL & IMES, MIT, Cambridge, MA 02139 Abstract tions based on observational data . Observational data is data which contains past actions, their outcomes, and possibly more context, bu" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1606_03976_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1606.03976_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "1606.03976_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1606_03976_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan ", 2015 ), with the distinction that we do not have access to the model generating the action. 1. Introduction Making predictions about causal effects of actions is a central problem in many domains. For example, a doctor deciding which medication will cause better outcomes for a patient; a government deciding who would benet most from subsidized job training; or a teacher deciding which study program would most benet a specic student. In this paper we focus on the problem of making these predicO" .

